After successfully applying this patch and recompiling the tty version
on a DecStation 5400, the program exhibits some rather strange
behaviour.  Note that the unpatched version ran great, though I did
have to work arround the usleep() call (nothing unusual to us Ultrix
type.)

1.  $(NO_USLEEP) must be added to CFLAGS in the Makefile (in addition
to uncommenting the NO_USLEEP definition) to compile on machines w/o
usleep().  When I compiled the origonal, unpatched version, I replaced
all calls to usleep() with napms() (dividing the parameter by 1000
since usleep takes values in microseconds and napms takes values in
milliseconds, of course) and linking to cursesX.

2.  After taking the first move (which can be either of the two valid
opening moves) the computer replies that it cannot take any moves,
that it gives up, that I won and that the game is over.

3.  The computer suggests illegal moves.  Actually, it suggests A8 as
the first move, which is the only time it is able to suggest anything.

4.  The patched version seems to randomly choose whether the computer
plays black or white.  I believe the computer always played black on
the unpatched version.  I think this should remain constant.

5.  Aesthetic changes:

    a.  The axes were most deffinately labeled properly in the
unpatched version with letters across the top and numbers down the
side.  This is only logical since coordinate systems are always
interpreted across-down and letters are usually given before numbers
(C3 instead of 3C).

    b.  It would be nice if (at least on the tty version) the text
"black" was replaced with "X" or "black (X)" and likewise for
white.

    c.  I like the machine's comments.  They should default to on.  I
especially like the one "you are doing good, now I will start
thinking" that comes up after particularly good moves.  Does this mean
that the game actually does play harder when it is behind?  (I suppose
I could look at the code, but hey, If I wanted to look at the code, I
would be posting the actual patch corrections instead of just
complaining that the program doesn't work ;-)

Since the majority of these problems seem logic related, I would tend
to think the same thing would happen when the program is compiled for
other output devices (though I have only tried the tty version) as
well as other hardware types, since the origonal version compiled and
ran fine.  Makes me wonder how a patch can be let out with some many
obvious problems...

